    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d819">

                <pb facs="0907" n="1741" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg1741"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 600.119/912</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        Argentina</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Stimson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-08-02T15:00:00-04:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">August 2, 1918, 3 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Your July 23, 3 p.m.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d819-f1"/> In December last arranged
                    with Compañía Transatlántica and Compañía Pinillos Lines to continue regular
                    services on basis of taking only cargo approved by War Trade Board. Later
                    Compañía Transatlántica signed bunker form B–4, which provides all their vessels
                    abide by Bureau of Transportation General Rules No. 1,<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d819-f2"/> of which clause (<hi rendition="#italic">l</hi>),
                    paragraph 5, requires approval War Trade Board to all voyages. See Journal No.
                    5. No new arrangements since then.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Polk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
